INSERT INTO `prd_idioma_x_itinerario` VALUES
(25,'en',NULL,25,'<br/>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-12 19:34:54',NULL,NULL),
(121,'en',NULL,120,'We head northwest from Quito, passing the equatorial monument and around the back side of Volcan Pichincha to reach the Mindo Cloud Forest Reserve. The climate here is much warmer than in Quito due to the lower altitude and coastal weather influence, creating a humid, subtropical cloud forest ecosystem that is home to a wide variety of unique flora and fauna. After a scenic drive to the town of Mindo and into the reserve, we begin a nature walk through this remarkable natural environment. As we hike, our naturalist guide identifies native species and teaches us the intricacies of the cloud forest ecosystem. Mindo is best known for its diverse bird life, and we comwill see scores of native birds, including cock-of-the-rocks, toucans, and more. We also observe a vast diversity of butterflies, orchids, and other native plants and animals. After our hike, we stop to visit the local waterfalls and enjoy a tasty lunch at a local restaurant before returning to Quito in the afternoon. <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n<br/></b>Private transportation, bilingual guide, and lunch. <br/><br/><b>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Restaurant beverages, and tips.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 00:54:38','ddiaz','2015-03-18 00:56:06'),
(123,'en',NULL,122,'From Quito we drive east through the Tumbaco valley, passing by several picturesque villages before reaching the eastern Andes and climbing up into Cayambe-Coca Nature Reserve. We continue to gain elevation and pass through highland paramo ecosystem until reaching the continental divide at 4,000 meters. From here we begin our descent into the Amazon cloud forest. We arrive at Papallacta in just under two hours. Before heading to the hot springs, we stop for a short visit at the local interpretation center and take a walk through a private cloud forest reserve, where we learn more about this unique ecosystem and seek out some of the native bird species.\n<br/><br/>After our nature walk, we make our way to Termas de Papallacta Spa &amp; Resort, where the spring water is mixed with frigid glacial river water to create thermal pools of varying temperatures. comHere, we spend hours soaking in these divine pools that range in temperature from frigid to scalding hot. The thermal waters are rich in minerals and sulfates, and are said to provide numerous health and beauty benefits. We can also set up optional spa treatments for the ultimate relaxation experience. After an indulgent day in the springs, we enjoy a gourmet lunch and head back to Quito in the afternoon. <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Private transportation, bilingual guide,\nhot springs, and lunch. <br/><br/><b>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Restaurant beverages and tips.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 01:30:04','ddiaz','2015-03-18 01:31:55'),
(124,'en',NULL,124,'We begin with a scenic drive around the residential\npart of Quito, passing several of the city´s beautiful\nparks and modern cultural highlights. We then\nhead to the old city center to begin a walking\ntour that will take us to the highlights of Quito´s\nbeautiful colonial town. Our first stop is Plaza de\nla Independencia, also known as Plaza Grande,\nthe heart of Quito´s old town. Plaza Grande is\nbordered with historic buildings on all sides,\nincluding Carondelet, the presidential palace,\nand the Metropolitan Cathedral.\n<br/><br/>Our next stop is La Compañia, regarded by many\nas the most beautiful church in Latin America. Its\nstone façade is handsome, but the real highlight\nis the interior. Full of intricate wood carvings\nand plaster work, and almost entirely covered in\ngold leaf, its opulence is hard to believe. Only\ntwo blocks away is San Francisco, another 16th\ncentury church. Although far less lavish than its\nneighbor, San Francisco is notable for its beautiful\nplaza and the Spanish architecture of its adjacent\nmonastery, which we enter and tour briefly.\n<br/><br/>After visiting a few more minor sites, we get\nback in our car and drive up to the Panecillo,\nwhere we enjoy a panoramic view of Quito and\nview the famous ´´Virgen de Quito´´ statue. We\nmake a quick stop at a fair trade artisan shop for\nsouvenir shopping before heading 30 kilometers\nnorth of the city to the Equatorial City Complex,\na collection of monuments and museums built\naround Latitude Zero. Here we visit the complex\ncalled Ciudad Mitad del Mundo, with its main\nattraction the Equatorial Monument. Additionally\nwe visit Intiñan Museum, a very fun and\npicturesque place that intents to show the equator\nfrom a different perspective, here you will find\nmany ancient solar calendars, clocks and season\nindicators. As well, you will be able to stand\non the ´´real equatorial line´´ and do all sorts of magical equator-like things such as balancing an\negg on the head of a nail, do a Strength test\ntrying not to fall sideways as you attempt to\nwalk the line with your eyes closed, see how the\nCoriolis effect works in both hemispheres, and\nmany more. The museum also displays interesting\nethnographical and cultural features. <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Private transportation, bilingual guide, museum\nentrance fee. <br/><br/><b>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Lunch and Tips. <br/><br/><b>NOTE\n</b><br/>The itinerary is subject to change without notice\ndue to seasonal changes, last-minute weather\nconditions, and guide?s criteria on benefit\nof our guest.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 01:41:23','ddiaz','2015-03-18 01:44:42'),
(130,'es',NULL,131,'<br/>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:28:24','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:28:26'),
(131,'es',NULL,130,'&nbsp;<br/>',NULL,NULL,'EXPLORE COTOPAXI NATIONAL PARK ON HORSEBACK',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:29:56','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:30:49'),
(132,'en',NULL,130,'We get an early start today, driving south from\nQuito through the imposing Avenue of the\nVolcanoes. If the weather is clear, we will see many\nlooming volcanoes along the road, including the\nsnowy peaks of several nevados. Our destination\nis Hacienda El Porvenir, a traditional hacienda\nthat has been in the same family for seven\ngenerations, famous for being the first in Ecuador\nto raise fighting bulls. After a warm cup of tea to\nfight off the cold, we have a brief riding lesson,\nthen gear up for the ride Chagra style, donning\nponchos, chaps, and hats.\n<br/><br/>We ride into Cotopaxi National Park and up\nthe foothills of Volcan Rumiñahui. As our guides\ntell us about native plants and Chagra culture,\nwe admire stunning views of the volcanic\nlandscapes, including the glaciated peak of\nCotopaxi towering above the high plain. We stop\nto view the hacienda´s fighting bulls and keep an\neye out for local wildlife as we ride. As long as\nthe weather cooperates, we are rewarded with\na spectacular view of Cotopaxi as we ride up\nthe slopes of Rumiñahui. With a bit more luck,\nwe may even get to admire the majestic and\nendangered Andean Condor. Box lunches will\nbe provided during the ride, and we spend the\nevening at a traditional colonial hacienda. (L, D)\n',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:30:53',NULL,NULL),
(133,'en',NULL,131,'We return to Cotopaxi National Park for the\nfirst part of the day, starting off with a hike to\nthe Pucara Inca ruins, where we learn about the\nhistory and culture of the western hemisphere´s\nmost powerful ancient civilization. We then move\non to Limpiopungo Glacier Lake at the base of\nRumiñahui, home to a wide variety of highland\nwaterfowl. Before exiting the national park, we\nvisit the Interpretation Center to learn more about\nCotopaxi´s native species and natural history.<br/><br/>We then drive into the foothills of Illinizas, a\npair of twin volcanic peaks across the road\nfrom Cotopaxi, where we find the trailhead of\na scenic highland cycling route. We hop on our\nbikes and descend down a dirt road that leads\ninto a beautiful valley and on to the small town\nof Chaupi. As we ride, we enjoy amazing views\nof the volcanoes Ilinizas, Corazon, Sincholagua,\nand others. We continue our ride past Chaupi\nand through fertile farmland, passing several old\ntrain stations and highland farms. After our ride,\nwe return to Quito in the afternoon. (B, L)<br/><br/><b>INCLUDED </b><br/>Private land transportation, accommodations in\ncolonial hacienda hotel, high-quality, name-brand\nmountain bikes and protective gear, horse rental\nwith poncho and chaps, bilingual adventure\nguide, all meals as indicated (B=Breakfast,\nL=Lunch, D=Dinner). <br/><b><br/>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Hotel and restaurant beverages, tips, national\npark entrance fee. <br/><br/><b>NOTE\n</b><br/>This itinerary is subject to change without notice\ndue to seasonal changes, inclement weather\nconditions, and the discretion of the guide. \n<br/><br/><b>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n<br/>TRAVEL TIME:</b> One and a half hours per day.\n<br/><b>SEASON:</b> Year-round.\n<br/><b>ALTITUDE:</b> 2,800 m to 4,300 m (9,400 to 14,000 ft.).<br/><b>WEATHER:</b> Weather in the high Andes varies depending on altitude. Daytime temperatures generally range between 47º to 75º F (8º to 24º C), and nighttime temperatures generally range between 40º to 50º F\n(5º to 10º C).\n<br/><b>SUGGESTED ITEMS TO PACK:</b> Warm, layered clothing, hiking boots, waterproof rain shell, sunglasses, sunblock.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:31:58','ddiaz','2015-03-18 02:33:44'),
(187,'es',NULL,185,'<br/>',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 20:56:12','ddiaz','2015-03-20 20:56:14'),
(188,'en',NULL,184,'We fly in from Quito this morning and check in to\nour boutique hotel in Cuenca´s historic city center.\nAfter settling in, we drive to Bibin, a small town\njust 20 minutes outside of Cuenca. Here we find ´´Cuenca Canopy,´´ a zip line adventure complex\nthat offers seven forest canopy zip lines ranging\nfrom 3 to 105 meters high and 85 to 350 meters\nlong. Professional guides and equipment ensure\nour safety as we zip through the canopy for two\nto three hours. We return to Cuenca for lunch\nand spend the afternoon exploring the historic\ncity center, famous for its well-preserved colonial\narchitecture, cobblestone streets, plazas, and\nchurches. We visit the highlights of downtown\nCuenca and learn about the iconic Panama hat\nduring a visit to the Museo del Sombrero. We\nalso stop at the handicrafts market, where we can\nbargain for beautiful handmade goods, including\ntextiles, leather goods, art, and much more. We\nreturn to our boutique hotel for dinner. (L, D)',NULL,NULL,'CANOPY ZIP LINE TOUR AND CUENCA CITY TOUR',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 20:56:33','ddiaz','2015-03-20 20:57:04'),
(190,'en',NULL,185,'Adrenaline and adventure are in store as we make\nour way to Rio Amarillo for a day of canyoning.\nAlso known as canyoneering, this adventure sport\ninvolves the use of various techniques to travel\nthrough river canyons, including rappelling,\nswimming, and scrambling. Rio Amarillo, only 20\nminutes outside of town on the flanks of Cabogana\nMountain, is a top-notch canyoning spot. After a\nscenic hike through farmland to reach the canyon,\nwe have a short instructional session and don our\ngear, including wetsuits, helmets, and harnesses.\nThe fun begins as we descend into Rio Amarillo´s\ncanyon. We zip down natural rock slides, dive\ninto deep pools, down-climb, scramble, swim,\nand rappel down seven waterfalls. Two expert guides lead us along this \nfive-hour journey, and rigorous safety practices are used to make sure\nthat our canyoning experience is exhilarating but\nalways safe. We return to Cuenca in time for a\nlate lunch and our flight back to Quito. (B, L) <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Accommodations in boutique hotel, private land\ntransportation, all meals as listed (B=breakfast,\nL=lunch, D=dinner), high-quality adventure\nequipment, bilingual adventure guide. <br/><br/><b>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Round trip airfare to and from Cuenca, hotel and\nrestaurant beverages, tips. <br/><br/><b>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n</b><br/><b>RECOMMENDED ITEMS TO PACK:</b> Waterproof\nhiking boots, waterproof gaiters, waterproof rain\njacket, warm layered clothing, non-cotton trekking\nclothes, bathing suit, camera, sunscreen,\ninsect repellant.',NULL,NULL,'CANYONING ADVENTURE',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 20:58:03','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:00:16'),
(191,'en',NULL,189,'Today we visit El Cajas National Park, one of\nCuenca´s top natural attractions. This highland\narea features a unique ´´paramo´´ ecosystem, a\nsort of tropical tundra endemic to the northern\nAndes. The paramo in El Cajas is notable for its\nextremely high humidity. The ground is covered\nin sponge-like vegetation and the park is home\nto over 200 lakes and lagoons. Our first stop is\nLaguna Llaviucu, one of the park´s most beautiful\nlagoons. As we hike around Llaviucu, we get\nintroduced to many of the region´s endemic\nspecies. We then carry on to Tres Cruces, the\npark´s highest point at 4,200 meters, where\nwe take in panoramic views of the landscape.\nOur final destination is Laguna La Toreadora,\nwhere we set out on a three-hour hike to\nexplore the small lagoons, beautiful landscapes,\nand endemic flora and fauna surrounding La\nToreadora. On our way back to town, we stop\nat Dos Chorreras, a colonial hosteria where we\nenjoy a hearty lunch of typical dishes, including\nEl Cajas´ famous trout. We return to Cuenca in\nthe late afternoon and enjoy a relaxing evening\nstrolling through town. (B, L, D)',NULL,NULL,'HIKE EL CAJAS NATIONAL PARK',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:08:09','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:15:48'),
(192,'en',NULL,187,'We take an early flight from Quito to Cuenca,\ntouching down in just over 45 minutes. Upon\narrival, we check in to our boutique hotel in\nCuenca´s old town and get ready to explore the\narea by bike. We start riding through Cuenca´s\ncolonial city center, enjoying the scenery of colonial\narchitecture, churches, and squares. We continue\nto Parque El Paraiso, a beautiful natural area near\ndowntown, then on to San Joaquin, a nearby\nvillage known for its handicrafts and gastronomy.\nAfter lunch and shopping in San Joaquin, we\nmake our way back to Cuenca, riding through\nnarrow streets and colonial neighborhoods. In the\nafternoon, we drive to nearby Baños, a suburb of\nCuenca famous for its natural volcanic hot springs.\nWe spend the rest of the afternoon relaxing in the\nluxurious Piedra de Agua hot springs, where we\nenjoy thermal pools, therapeutic mud baths, and a\ngourmet dinner. (L, D)',NULL,NULL,'BIKE CUENCA\'S OLD TOWN, BAÑOS HOT SPRINGS',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:10:32','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:16:31'),
(193,'en',NULL,188,'This morning, we drive into the countryside\nsurrounding Cuenca and on to a nearby colonial\nhacienda. After an introduction to the grounds\nand a short riding lesson, we get on our horses\nand set out for a three-hour ride through the rural\nAndean countryside. We ride at a relaxed pace\nalong beautiful trails, passing through ancient\nvillages, small farms, and idyllic landscapes. After\nour ride, we enjoy a delicious lunch and finish\nour day with a short walk around the hacienda.\nWe drive back to Cuenca in the afternoon and\ncatch our flight back to Quito. (B, L) \n<br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Accommodations in boutique hotel, private land\ntransportation, all meals as listed (B=breakfast,\nL=lunch, D=dinner), high-quality biking and riding\nequipment, bilingual naturalist guide.\n<br/><br/><b>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Round trip airfare to and from Cuenca, hotel\nand restaurant beverages, tips.\n<br/><br/><b>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n<br/>RECOMMENDED ITEMS TO PACK:</b> Waterproof\nhiking boots, waterproof gaiters, waterproof rain\njacket, warm layered clothing, non-cotton trekking\nclothes, bathing suit, camera, sunscreen,\ninsect repellant.',NULL,NULL,'COLONIAL HACIENDA HORSEBACK RIDE',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:13:12','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:14:09'),
(194,'en',NULL,191,'Today we explore Cuenca´s famous old town by\nbike. The center of Cuenca is designated as a\nUNESCO World Heritage Site for its well-preserved colonial architecture. As we bike down narrow\ncobblestone streets, we learn about Cuenca´s\ncolorful history and take in views of picturesque\nplazas, colonial churches, and beautiful parks.\nFrom downtown, we continue to Parque El\nParaiso, a natural area located just south of the\nriver Tomebamba. We then head out of the city\nand on to San Joaquin, a small, nearby village\nknown for its handicrafts and gastronomy. After\nshopping and lunch at San Joaquin, we head back\nto our hotel in Cuenca´s center, passing through\ntraditional residential neighborhoods along the\nway. In the afternoon, we have some free time\nto explore Cuenca on foot before heading to the\nairport for our flight back to Quito. (B, L)\n<br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Accommodations in boutique hotel, private land\ntransportation, all meals as listed (B=breakfast,\nL=lunch, D=dinner), high-quality climbing and\nbiking equipment, bilingual adventure guide,\nprofessional climbing guide.\n<br/><br/><b>NOT INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Round trip airfare to and from Cuenca, hotel\nand restaurant beverages, tips.\n<br/><b><br/>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n</b><br/><b>RECOMMENDED ITEMS TO PACK: </b>Waterproof\nhiking boots, waterproof rain jacket, warm\nlayered clothing, non-cotton trekking clothes,\ncamera, sunscreen, insect repellant.',NULL,NULL,'EXPLORE CUENCA\'S OLD TOWN BY BIKE',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:22:03','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:26:54'),
(195,'en',NULL,190,'We catch an early flight from Quito and check\nin to our boutique hotel in downtown Cuenca.\nWe then drive to Cojitambo, Ecuador´s best\nrock climbing spot, just 30 minutes away from\nCuenca. This mountain held great significance for\nthe pre-Inca Cañari civilization due to its strategic\nlocation overlooking wide valleys to the East and\nWest. From the top, there are beautiful panoramic\nviews of the city of Azogues and rolling Andean\ncountryside, as well as a few small Cañari ruins.\nCojitambo has a lot of vertical rock, and we can\nchoose from several areas and routes of many\ngrades depending on your climbing experience.\nMost beginners choose the west side of Cojitambo,\nhome to more moderate routes and walls up to\n60 meters tall. The climbing here doesn´t require\nany previous experience, and seasoned climbing\nguides will assist you in learning safety and\nmovement techniques as you climb. After a fun\nday on the rock, we return to Cuenca in the late\nafternoon. (L, D)',NULL,NULL,'ROCK CLIMBING AT COJITAMBO',NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:22:30','ddiaz','2015-03-20 21:23:02'),
(220,'en',NULL,217,'We begin our whitewater adventure with an\nearly start, departing from Quito at 7:00am\nand driving west. We pass through the inter-\nAndean valley, then begin to gain altitude until\nwe reach the continental divide at 4,000 meters\nabove sea level. Here we begin our descent\ntoward the Amazon basin, passing through\nhighland paramo and cloud forest ecosystems\nuntil we reach the Chaco gorge put-in at 2,200\nmeters. We have a short safety briefing before\ntaking to the water. Over the next 18 kilometers,\nwe will run the best section of Class IV rapids\naccessible from Quito. The run is sustained, with\nmedium volume and a combination of technical\nand powerful paddling. The Quijos has a unique\nfeel, as the water and climate are much cooler\nthan Ecuador´s Amazonian rivers, giving you a\ntrue Andean paddling experience. When we´re\nnot digging deep to get past the next Class IV\nrapid, we enjoy the scenery of the Chaco gorge and spot many native bird species in the trees.\nWe enjoy a tasty and nutritious riverfront picnic\nlunch at our take-out and return to Quito in the\nlate afternoon. <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Private ground transportation, high-quality rafting\nequipment and personal safety gear, lunch\nand snacks, certified rafting guide. <br/><br/><b>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n</b><br/><b>TRAVEL TIME:</b> Two hours each way.\n<br/><b>RAFTING TIME:</b> Three hours.\nSEASON: November to April.\n<br/><b>ALTITUDE:</b> 5,900 ft. to 7,200 ft. (1800 m to 2200 m).\n<br/><b>WEATHER:</b> Daytime temperatures in the area average 46º F to 72º F (8º C to 22º C)\n<br/><b>SUGGESTED ITEMS TO PACK:</b> Water sandals, waterproof sunblock and mosquito repellent.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 22:53:21','ddiaz','2015-03-20 22:55:28'),
(234,'en',NULL,231,'We depart from Quito in the morning and head south, driving through the Avenue of the Volcanoes.Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed eiusmod \ntempor incidunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim \nveniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquid ex ea \ncommodi consequat.','Quito','Cotopaxi','Caminata en Limpiopungo','Full Day','<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 23:50:05','jmora','2016-10-04 22:43:06'),
(235,'en',NULL,233,'We begin our adventure driving through southern\nQuito until we reach Lloa, a small colonial town on\nthe outskirts of the city. From Lloa, we turn toward\nPichincha and start driving up, gaining altitude\nas we drive along rural roads with incredible\nvistas of Quito below. Pichincha looms directly\nover the city, and we enjoy panoramic views\nthat span from the residential neighborhoods\nin the south all the way to the old airport in the\nnorth, with many of the city´s highlights visible\nfrom above. Although we´re only minutes away\nfrom the bustling metropolis, the environment\nchanges dramatically as we gain elevation. We pass through the paramo vegetation line at the\n3,600 meter level and continue climbing through\ndramatic canyons and ravines until we reach the\nend of the road. Here we begin our hike toward\nthe rim of Guagua Pichincha. Our guide teaches\nus about the endemic paramo ecosystem and\nlocal wildlife, as well as the natural history and\ngeology of this life-giving volcano. The air gets\ncolder and the native plants smaller and hardier\nas we climb. Weather and fitness permitting, we\ncontinue all the way to the summit and crater\nrim at 4,874 meters. After our hike, we enjoy a\nhealthy and delicious picnic lunch on the slopes\nand return to Quito in the afternoon. <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Bilingual adventure guide, private transportation,\nnutritious picnic lunch and snacks. <br/><br/><b>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n</b><br/><b>TRAVEL TIME:</b> One hour each way.\n<br/><b>HIKING TIME: </b>Three to four hours.\n<br/><b>SEASON:</b> Year-round.\n<br/><b>ALTITUDE:</b> 3600 to 4874 meters (11,800\nto 15,991 feet).\n<br/><b>SUGGESTED ITEMS TO PACK:</b> Layered clothing\nfor hot and cold weather, sunblock, gloves, boots,\nwaterproof rain gear, hiking shoes, binoculars. ',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-20 23:58:39','ddiaz','2015-03-21 00:00:42'),
(241,'en',NULL,239,'Driving south from Quito, we arrive at the\nentrance of Pasochoa Wildlife Refuge in only\nan hour. The refuge is located inside the huge\ncrater of Pasochoa Volcano. Time and eruptions\nhave collapsed Pasochoa´s crater and cone,\nleaving the volcano with a horseshoe shape. This\ngeological formation gives the area a unique\necosystem, as the crater collects warm air and\nhumidity, creating an isolated microclimate that is\nhome to many native species. Inside the collapsed\ncrater are several different types of cloud forest,\neach one with its own set of native highland\ntrees and shrubs, as well as orchids, bromeliads,\nlichens, and ferns. This lush vegetation is home\nto abundant bird life. Over 100 native bird\nspecies have been identified, including at least\n11 species of hummingbirds. As we hike through\nvarious parts of the reserve, we can observe the\nvariations in vegetation with changes in elevation\nand humidity. If the skies are clear, we also enjoy\na stunning backdrop of snow-capped volcanic\npeaks in the distance. We can modify the route\nbased on guest preferences, and the hike can\ntake anywhere from three to six hours depending\non the route you choose. We enjoy a delicious\nand scenic picnic lunch within the reserve and\nhead back to Quito in the afternoon. <br/><br/><b>INCLUDED\n</b><br/>Bilingual adventure guide, private transportation,\nnutritious picnic lunch and snacks.\n<br/><br/><b>IMPORTANT INFORMATION\n<br/>TRAVEL TIME:</b> One hour each way.\n<br/><b>HIKING TIME: </b>Three to six hours.\n<br/><b>SEASON:</b> Year-round.\n<br/><b>ALTITUDE:</b> 2800 to 3800 meters (9,200 to\n12,500 feet).\n<br/><b>SUGGESTED ITEMS TO PACK:</b> Layered clothing for\nhot and cold weather, sunblock, gloves, boots,\nwaterproof rain gear, hiking shoes, binoculars.',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'<br/>','ddiaz','2015-03-21 00:23:42','ddiaz','2015-03-21 00:27:59'),
